The section adds a `where_in` Jinja filter that renders user-controlled filter values into a raw SQL `IN (...)` clause. The only escaping it performs is doubling the `mark` character (default `'`) inside string values. This is a SQL-injection sink, not an authorization (CWE-639/862/863) flaw, and the retrieved policy excerpts contain no explicit rule governing template filter escaping or authorization for this code path. Per the rules, I report only authorization flaws with cited policy, so there is no confirmed candidate here.

